/*
	RMSTEST 13 — is elevation's ~3% overshoot caused by GROWTH, or by elevation
	scaling differently from objects?

	preview-design Sec.15 item 7d. RMSTEST_10 measured elevation tile counts of
	853 and 851 against a budget of 829, and 428 against 414 — about 3% high in
	all three. Sec.4 currently blames growth overshoot. That is a hypothesis
	fitted to two data points, and at least two other explanations survive:

	  (i)   growth overshoot — the clump adds tiles in batches and overshoots
	        when it crosses its target.
	  (ii)  elevation scales differently from objects. RMSTEST_11/12 measured
	        the OBJECT path (set_scaling_to_map_size) and found exact area then
	        truncation. Sec.4 asserts set_scale_by_size follows the same rule,
	        but that has never been measured.
	  (iii) the engine raises a skirt of extra tiles to render the hill edge.

	THIS MAP REMOVES SCALING ENTIRELY. `number_of_tiles 829` with NO
	set_scale_by_size means the budget is unambiguously 829 — the exact number
	RMSTEST_10's scaled run was working from — with the scaling path deleted.

	READ IT WITH THE PROBE — the elevation histogram, tiles at level 1
	  ~853 (about 3% over)  -> GROWTH overshoots, independent of scaling.
	                           Explanation (i). Sec.4's attribution is right,
	                           and ~3% becomes a real [tune] value for Sec.6.2
	                           since our own growth stops exactly at target.
	  exactly 829           -> growth is EXACT, so the 3% lives in elevation's
	                           scaling path. Explanation (ii): set_scale_by_size
	                           does not follow Sec.4's rule, and Sec.6.2/6.4's
	                           budgets need measuring separately from objects.
	  something else        -> report it; both current explanations are wrong.

	THEN RUN IT AGAIN WITH number_of_tiles 25 (edit this file, as with
	RMSTEST_10). That separates explanation (iii):
	  ~26 (about 3% over)   -> proportional to area. Consistent with (i).
	  ~40+ (way over)       -> a perimeter-driven skirt. A 25-tile blob has a
	                           perimeter around 18, so a skirt is an enormous
	                           fraction of a small clump and a rounding-error
	                           fraction of a large one. Explanation (iii).

	DESIGN NOTES
	  Identical to RMSTEST_10 except that set_scale_by_size is gone and the
	  declared value is the post-scaling number — one variable changed.
	  MaxHeight 1 so every raised tile is level 1 and the histogram is a single
	  bucket. One clump, so no budget division. Player lands tiny and pushed to
	  the rim, since elevation avoids player-land origins by about 9 tiles
	  (guide:1169) and could otherwise eat into the budget.

	RUN ON: Small, 2 players. Map size does not matter to the arithmetic now
	that scaling is gone, but keep it Small so the comparison with RMSTEST_10
	is like for like.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
base_terrain GRASS

create_player_lands
{
	terrain_type GRASS
	base_size 3
	number_of_tiles 1
	circle_radius 45 0
}

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>

create_elevation 1
{
	base_terrain GRASS
	number_of_tiles 25
	number_of_clumps 1
}
